        <section id="overview" class="intro">
          <h1>
            Characterise the motors
            <br />
            <em>
              one effect at a time.
            </em>
          </h1>
          <p class="lead">
            The motors on your robot will likely behave in unexpected ways.  The motor subsystem contains physical and electronic effects that are not obvious from the software commands alone. We need to develop a description of the motor performance, called <strong>characterisation</strong>.
          </p>
          <figure class="orientation-figure">
            <img src="assets/images/motors/motor_encoder.png" alt="Illustration of a disassembled motor" width="100%" />
          </figure>
          <p class="lead">
            Your first job is to construct an evidence-backed explanation of the motors subsystem response.  This means you will need to investigate how each motor behaves, take measurements, and plot graphs.</p>
            <p>
           In these exercises, the code you will write will be very simple: you will need to activate a motor for some time and record some measurements.  Some aspects could be automated with <strong>iteration</strong> (e.g., loops).  However, it will be important to think about whether using iteration may also impact the observed motor performance.  You will need to design and write different test cases to measure different characteristics.  You will need to think carefully about any assumptions.</p>
           <p>
           To get started writing code for your robot, the first exercise will guide you to review the <strong>StampC3_Template</strong> code that has the example behaviour of line following.</p>
           <p>It might surprise you that, even though we are focusing on <strong>motor output</strong>, we are still going to learn to use a <strong>sensor</strong>.  </p>
           <p>The simplest way to determine the speed of the robot might be to set the motor power, allow the robot to travel a known fixed distance, and record the time it takes from beginning to end.  We would then divide the distance by the time to work out the velocity, for example, in meters per second.  </p>
           <p>Taking <strong>external measurements</strong> like this (external to the robot) are perfectly valid, and sometimes essential.  However, a robotic system will also have many sensors which can be used to take more <strong>internal measurements</strong> (internal to the robot), with very good control of time and other aspects. In the following exercises, we will learn to command the motors and to measure their activity via quadrature encoders.</p>
          </p>
