          <article id="exercise-01" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">
              01
            </div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div>
                  <span class="concept-tag">
                    Controller orientation · code trace
                  </span>
                  <h2>
                    Command the motors from the StampC3
                  </h2>
                </div>
                <span class="time-badge">
                  20–30 min
                </span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  <strong>Goal:</strong> establish that you can use code to deliberately start and stop simple motor movements.<br><br> 
                  
                  After following the <a href="getting-started.html">Getting Started</a> exercises, you should have a renamed copy of the <strong>StampC3_Template</strong> on your computer - this is a good starting point.  Reviewing the files, the <code>.ino</code> file is the <strong>top-level</strong> file - this means it essentially contains and structures all other code used.  Therefore, the .ino file is the correct starting point to review and understand the code.</p>
                  <p>The original StampC3_Template has an example line-following controller that uses surface-sensor information to make decisions to one command each motor.  This is more complicated than we currently need, but it serves as a good example to recognise how to use functions, and the patterns of code that produce robot control.</p>
                  <p>When we develop our later test routines we will also need to decide when to start and stop the motors.  As a first exercise, the easiest way to start is to edit existing code.  We want to build some familiarity with the code used to operate the robot. </p>
                <p>
                  In <strong>Controller.h</strong>, find every call to <strong>robot.setMotorPWM(...)</strong>. Follow the path from <strong>update(...)</strong> into <strong>runLineFollower(...)</strong>. Compare the commands used while the robot is waiting, following the line, searching for it, and stopped. <br><br>
                  
                  Inspect <strong>Robot.h</strong>: find the <strong>setMotorPWM(float l, float r)</strong> declaration, the motor values it stores, and the I2C request that sends the command to the robot.
                  <br><br>
                  In these exercises, it is <strong>strongly recommended that you keep your robot upside-down on the table</strong> so that the robot does not run away at high speed and <strong>cause damage</strong>.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="exercise-grid">

                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Motor command orientation guidance">
                    <button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">
                      Self-Directed Route
                    </button>
                    <button class="context-tab" data-context="ai" role="tab" aria-selected="false">
                      AI-Assisted Route
                    </button>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel active" data-panel="nonai">
<h3>
                    Investigation brief
                  </h3>
                  <ol>
                  <li><strong>Get familiar with the code:</strong> In the above description, the structure of the code is described as having a top level .ino file, which makes a call to the <strong>controller.update(...)</strong> function within Controller.h.  The Controller.h code makes function calls to the Robot.h file.  The code in Robot.h has dependency on i2c_datatypes.h.  As a first exercise, quickly sketch a block diagram of the organisation of the source code files.  Look to sketch the relationships, such as when code is nested within other code.  Don't worry too much about making this "correct" yet - as you continue to explore the code you can come back to correct your understanding.</li>
<li>			    <strong>Verify the example works:</strong> Before making any changes, it is best practice to first verify an example works.  This way, we will know if our changes have caused a fault or not.  Run the supplied line-following example and observe how the robot behaves when it is waiting, follows the line, and searches after losing it. You should be able to observe a sequence of behaviours.  Now inspect the example code.  Read the code to trace the execution path that reaches each <strong>robot.setMotorPWM(...)</strong> call.  Try to navigate each 'if' statement by considering possible alternative conditional values.  You can think of this as building a mental map of the logic of the code, and how it relates to robot behaviours.
                    </li>
                    <li>
			    <strong>Start by modifying existing code:</strong> Using the runLineFollower(...) function as a template, create a new function with a new name, such as motorTest(...).  You could start by copy-pasting the runLineFollower(..) function, renaming it, and then calling your new function from update().  Remember to remove the call in update() to the original runLineFollower(...) function, or both will run.  
<ol>			    
			    
			    <li><strong>Verify</strong> that the same line following behaviour is produced from the renamed function.  <strong>Modify this renamed function</strong>, look to demonstrate a controlled start and stop.  It is recommended to keep the button press and signal functionality to start the robot in a controlled manner.
			    
			    <li>An easy first approach is the use the Arduino function <code>delay(...)</code> (<a href="https://docs.arduino.cc/language-reference/en/functions/time/delay/">reference page</a>) between your two motor command calls.  For example, to send power to the motors, delay for 2 seconds, then to send 0 power to the motors to stop.  However, this will <strong>block</strong> your code - meaning that your robot will not run any other code until delay(...) has finished.  Using this approach will make things difficult later: you won't be able to easily report the measurements you need back to your computer.</li>
			    <li>A better approach is to adopt a <strong>non-blocking</strong> convention.  Review the example code and find where <strong>TaskTimer_c</strong> has been setup and used.  TaskTimer_c has been provided to you to help your write non-blocking code.  You can also review the technical reference for TaskTimer_c <a href="./stampc3-reference.html#task-timer">here</a>.  Review the example code to discover the implementation pattern when using TaskTimer_c:
			    <ol>
			    <li>Create an instance of TaskTimer_c.</li>
			    <li>How to configure the time interval of the TaskTimer_c instance.</li>
			    <li>How to check if the TaskTimer_c is ready, run a function, and reset the TaskTimer_c.</li></ol> </li>
			    </ol>
                    </li>
			    
			    <li>Update your code from the previous task to make the robot rotate on the spot for approximately ten seconds before stopping it deliberately.  This should not be difficult.  In these first exercises, we are just looking to explore and understand how the robot.setMotorPWM(...) function behaves.  In this case, explore what happens if a mix of positive and negative values are sent to the motors.  When does the robot rotate on the spot?  What other behaviours does the robot exhibit?
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      Demonstrate a speed ramp: issue a sequence of increasing motor-command magnitudes, then decreasing magnitudes, then a stop command. Similar to the previous task, you ideally want to achieve <strong>non-blocking</strong> code.  To achieve this, you will need to create some variables so that your controller can "remember" information between calls to the update(...) function.  Review the example code: the Controller_c class has some variables declared in both the <strong>public</strong> and <strong>private</strong> sections of the class template.
                      <ol>
                      <li>Create either a public or private variable to "remember" the last power value sent to the motors.</li>
                      <li>Use your controller function to increment this value periodically, by using a TaskTimer_c instance</li>
                      <li>Create another public or private variable to "remember" if the power value should be going up each time, or down.</li>
                      <li>Choose the command levels, timing, and what counts as a safe test.  Implement if statements to ensure the robot stays within these constraints.</li>
                      </ol>
                    </li>
                    <li>If update() did call runLineFollower(...) and your new function, what would you expect to see?  If you are curious, you can try this and observe what happens.
                    <ol>
                    <li>Try switching the order of runLineFollower(...) and your new function - what happens then?</li>
                    <li>If the result is surprising to you, discuss it with your peers and a member of teaching staff.</li></ol>
</li>                  
                  </ol>


                    <strong>
                      Expected Outcome: Build a first movement routine
                    </strong>
                    <p>
                      After following these exercises, you should have written a simple controller that is able to periodically update the power sent to the robot motors.  Your simple controller should also be able to start and stop the robot in a controlled manner.  
                    </p>
                   
                  </div>
