          <article id="exercise-02" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">
              02
            </div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div>
                  <span class="concept-tag">
                    Encoder data · speed measurement
                  </span>
                  <h2>
                    Measuring Wheel Speed: Encoders
                  </h2>
                </div>
                <span class="time-badge">
                  25–35 min
                </span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  Motor commands alone do not tell you how quickly a wheel moves. The robot provides encoder counts: a count is a record of wheel rotation, so the change in count over a known time interval gives a repeatable speed measurement in encoder counts per second. This is a useful first speed unit because it does not require you to assume a wheel radius or encoder calibration before you have investigated them.
                </p>
                <p>
                  In <strong>Robot.h</strong>, find <strong>getEncoders()</strong>, <strong>getLeftEncoderCount()</strong>, and <strong>getRightEncoderCount()</strong>. The first refreshes the cached encoder snapshot; the named getters then make the left and right values explicit. Take two refreshed readings around a timed interval and calculate <strong>encoder speed = (final count − initial count) / elapsed time</strong>. The sign and size of the change are evidence about direction and rate of rotation.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Continue in the same sketch and the same <code>Controller_c</code> used in Exercise 1. The supplied <code>update(...)</code> function already calls <code>robot.getEncoders()</code> every accepted update cycle, before it calls the selected controller routine. Your new routine can therefore read the latest cached counts without adding another I2C request. Keep the movement and measurement decisions in the controller routine; in Phase 1, you may report the resulting values in any clear form that works for you.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="exercise-grid">

                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Encoder speed measurement guidance">
                    <button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">
                      Self-Directed Route
                    </button>
                    <button class="context-tab" data-context="ai" role="tab" aria-selected="false">
                      AI-Assisted Route
                    </button>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel active" data-panel="nonai">
<h3>
                    Investigation brief
                  </h3>
                  <ol>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Start from Exercise 1.</strong> Keep your working movement routine so that you can return to it. In <code>Controller.h</code>, create a new function with a descriptive name such as <code>measureWheelSpeed(Robot_c &amp;robot, unsigned long now)</code>.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Select the new test.</strong> In <code>update(...)</code>, replace the call to the previous test function with a call to <code>measureWheelSpeed(robot, now)</code>. Leave only one test function active. Continue to use the existing button and <code>signal</code> arrangement so that the wheels cannot start unexpectedly.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Build a non-blocking measurement.</strong> Add controller variables to remember the test phase, initial count, initial time, and final count. Use a <code>TaskTimer_c</code> to move through settling, measuring, and finished phases. Read the cached count with <code>robot.getLeftEncoderCount()</code> or <code>robot.getRightEncoderCount()</code>, and command or stop the wheel with <code>robot.setMotorPWM(...)</code>.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Run a physical trial.</strong> With the robot safely supported so that its wheels can turn freely, command one wheel at a known, modest PWM while the other remains at zero. Allow a short settling period, record the first count, measure for a fixed interval, record the second count, and deliberately stop both wheels. Repeat for the other wheel and for reverse rotation.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Calculate and plot.</strong> Keep the PWM, direction, elapsed time, initial count, final count, and calculated speed together. Repeat the same trial and plot or tabulate the measured encoder speeds. Choose an interval that produces a clear count change without hiding how the response changes after starting.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Check the outcome.</strong> Your code should start, measure, and stop without <code>delay(...)</code>; the selected wheel should turn in the requested direction; and your evidence should contain raw counts, elapsed time, calculated speed, and repeat measurements. The precise speed and repeat-to-repeat variation may differ between teams.
                    </li>
                  </ol>
                  <strong>Pseudocode before C++</strong>
                  <pre><code>WHEN the test starts:
    command one wheel and begin the settling interval
WHEN settling finishes:
    remember the cached encoder count and start time
WHEN the measurement interval finishes:
    remember the final count and time
    command both motors to stop
    calculate and report the average encoder speed</code></pre>
                  <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Encoder speed equals final count minus initial count, divided by elapsed time in seconds">
                    <span>encoder speed</span><b>=</b><span>(final count − initial count) / elapsed time in seconds</span>
                  </div>
                  <p>
                    <strong>Flow-diagram placeholder:</strong> start signal → command → settle → first snapshot → timed measurement → second snapshot → stop → calculate.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    <a class="template-link placeholder-link" href="#" aria-disabled="true" onclick="return false;">Example Python plotting notebook — link to be added</a>
                  </p>
                  <strong>Expected outcome: a reusable speed measurement</strong>
                  <p>
                    You should now have a named, non-blocking test function that converts two encoder snapshots into counts per second. Later exercises reuse this measurement rather than inventing a different robot interface.
                  </p>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel" data-panel="ai" hidden>
