          <article id="exercise-03" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">
              03
            </div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div>
                  <span class="concept-tag">
                    Threshold discovery · static test
                  </span>
                  <h2>
                    Deadband: find where motion begins
                  </h2>
                </div>
                <span class="time-badge">
                  25–35 min
                </span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  A motor can receive a non-zero command without producing detectable sustained wheel motion. This low-command region is called the
                  <strong>
                    deadband
                  </strong>
                  . It can arise from static friction in the motor and gearbox, bearing and tyre friction, a minimum effective PWM duty cycle, quantisation, or small thresholds in the driver and control electronics.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Deadband is a threshold phenomenon, so begin from rest. The command at which the wheel first starts to move is not necessarily the same as the command needed to keep an already-moving wheel turning. The threshold may also differ between the left and right wheels or between forward and reverse motion. Treat each direction as a measurement to be tested, not as an assumption to be copied.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Your first task is therefore not to estimate speed or fit a line. It is to identify the interval of commands that produces no reliable sustained motion, estimate the first-motion threshold, and quantify how repeatable that threshold is.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Reuse the timed encoder-speed method from Exercise 2. A new test function can change the requested PWM between trials while the same <code>robot.setMotorPWM(...)</code>, cached encoder getters, and <code>TaskTimer_c</code> pattern collect comparable evidence. Any deadband effect, and its magnitude, may vary between teams, wheels, and directions.
                </p>
              </div>
