              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  Every actuator has a finite operating range.
                  <strong>
                    Saturation
                  </strong>
                  occurs when increasing the command no longer produces a corresponding increase in the physical output. For a mobile robot, this may appear as a plateau in settled wheel speed: commands above a certain level all produce approximately the same speed.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Saturation can have several causes. The command may be clipped by software or limited by the available PWM range. The motor driver may be unable to apply more voltage or current. The motor and gearbox may reach a physical speed or torque limit, or the battery and power system may be unable to supply the demanded current under load. Friction, wheel contact, floor surface, and payload can also change where the plateau appears.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Distinguish
                  <strong>
                    command saturation
                  </strong>
                  from
                  <strong>
                    motor-system saturation
                  </strong>
                  . Command saturation means that the requested command itself has been clipped or capped. Motor-system saturation means that the command continues to increase, but the measured wheel output stops responding proportionally. Your experiment should vary the command far enough to distinguish these possibilities, while recording the physical conditions and measurement limits.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Identify saturation before studying response shape. The deadband threshold provides a lower boundary for useful motion, and the saturation region provides an upper boundary. Between them is a sensible working range in which gain and non-linearity can be estimated without fitting effects caused by “not moving” or “unable to go any faster.”
                </p>
                <p>
                  Extend the command-sweep structure from Exercise 3, but replace the motion/no-motion classification with the settled encoder-speed measurement from Exercise 2. The cached <code>robot.getLeftMotorPWM()</code> and <code>robot.getRightMotorPWM()</code> values can help you record the command that your controller requested; the encoders provide the separate physical response. The position and visibility of any plateau may vary between teams.
                </p>
