              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  A relationship is
                  <strong>
                    linear
                  </strong>
                  when a graph of output against input can be described by a straight line over the range being studied. For a motor, this would mean that equal increases in command produce approximately equal increases in settled wheel speed. A
                  <strong>
                    non-linear
                  </strong>
                  response is one whose scale changes across the operating range: the graph may flatten, steepen, bend, or contain different regions with different slopes.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Non-linearity is common in robotics because the command passes through several physical and computational stages. Motor torque, electrical resistance, gearbox friction, wheel slip, load, and control or quantisation effects can each make the relationship depart from a straight line. A curved graph is therefore an observation to explain, not automatically evidence for one particular mechanism.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Before choosing a model, test the assumptions that make a simple response model useful. Is the response monotonic, so that increasing command does not reduce speed? Is it repeatable under the same conditions? Is the curve similar for both wheels and both rotation directions? Does the shape remain stable when the robot is placed on another surface or carries a different load? Also check that the data exclude the deadband and saturation regions, since those effects can create apparent curvature of their own.
                </p>
                <p>
                  One candidate model is a normalised power law. Let
                  <em>
                    x
                  </em>
                  be the command scaled between the lower and upper limits of the usable range, and let
                  <em>
                    y
                  </em>
                  be the corresponding normalised settled speed. The parameter
                  <strong>
                    gamma
                  </strong>
                  controls the curvature:
                </p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Normalised power-law model: y equals x raised to the power gamma">
                  <span>
                    normalised speed
                  </span>
                  <b>
                    =
                  </b>
                  <span>
                    normalised command
                  </span>
                  <sup>
                    γ
                  </sup>
                </div>
                <p>
                  When γ = 1, the model is linear. When γ is greater than 1, the response is relatively flatter at low commands and rises more sharply at high commands; when γ is less than 1, it rises more sharply at low commands and flattens later. Gamma is a compact description of the measured shape, not a claim that the motor contains a single physical component called “gamma.” Compare this model with a straight-line model using residuals and a held-out prediction.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Use the same robot commands and encoder-speed measurements as the gain investigation. The programming change is mainly to sample the usable range more completely and retain the measurements needed to compare models. A response may look linear, weakly curved, or strongly curved, and its shape may vary between teams, wheels, directions, and physical conditions.
                </p>
