              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  A symmetric motor model would predict that reversing the command reverses the direction of motion while preserving the response magnitude. Real mobile robots often violate this assumption.
                  <strong>
                    Forward/reverse asymmetry
                  </strong>
                  means that matched commands in opposite directions produce systematically different thresholds, gains, speeds, or dynamic responses.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Several mechanisms can contribute: the two directions may load the gearbox differently, the motor and driver may not behave identically under reversed current, the wheel may contact the floor differently while moving forwards or backwards, and the robot’s centre of mass or trailing cables may introduce a directional load. A difference that repeats under the same conditions is a systematic effect, not random noise.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Treat this exercise as a provocation to revisit earlier assumptions. Did you assume that the forward deadband was the same as the reverse deadband? Did one gain estimate represent both directions? Did the saturation limit and non-linear response shape transfer when the command sign changed? A good model should state where symmetry is supported by evidence and where separate directional parameters are needed.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Rather than create a new measurement method, pair positive and negative commands of the same magnitude in a new test function. Use the same <code>robot.setMotorPWM(...)</code>, cached encoder getters, timing, and settling rules used earlier so that direction is the deliberate change. The direction and size of any asymmetry may vary between teams and may not be visually obvious.
                </p>
