              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  <strong>
                    Noise
                  </strong>
                  is short-timescale random fluctuation around an underlying response. For example, if the same command is applied repeatedly, the measured wheel speed may move slightly above and below its typical value. Noise can arise in sensors, encoders, motor current, electrical supplies, timing, communication, or the mechanical contact between wheel and floor.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>
                    Variation
                  </strong>
                  describes the spread of outcomes across repeated trials. Repeatability is how consistently the system produces the same result when the operating conditions are repeated. We can quantify repeatability using a range, standard deviation, percentile interval, or another suitable statistic. High spread means low repeatability; low spread means high repeatability.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Do not assume that the amount of noise is constant everywhere. At one operating point, measurements might cluster tightly; at another, they might be widely spread. The spread may depend on command magnitude, wheel speed, direction, load, or proximity to deadband and saturation. This is sometimes called
                  <em>
                    heteroscedasticity
                  </em>
                  : the variability changes with the operating condition.
                </p>
                <p>
                  A useful model should therefore state where it applies. Ask whether the mean response changes, whether the variability changes, and whether successive errors are independent. A fixed additive-noise model may be adequate over one range but misleading over the whole motor operating envelope.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Turn an earlier settled-speed trial into a repeated-trial routine rather than writing another measurement method. The controller should use the same commands and timing while a trial counter changes. Automation helps consistency, but it does not guarantee independence: battery state, temperature, wheel contact, and the order of trials may still matter. The amount and pattern of variation may differ between teams.
                </p>
