              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  Robotic actuators have
                  <strong>
                    dynamics
                  </strong>
                  : their output changes over time rather than jumping instantly to the value requested by a command. Motor winding inductance, rotor and wheel inertia, gearbox friction, changing back EMF, load, and the timing of embedded control updates can all contribute to a delayed or gradual response.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>
                    Slew-rate limiting
                  </strong>
                  is a limit on how quickly the response can change. If a wheel speed cannot increase faster than a maximum rate, a step in command produces a ramp rather than an immediate jump. Rising and falling responses may have different rate limits, so measure both where practical.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>
                    Lag
                  </strong>
                  describes a response that approaches its target gradually. A common first-order model is:
                </p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="First-order lag equation: the rate of change of speed equals the difference between target and current speed divided by the time constant">
                  <span>
                    dv/dt
                  </span>
                  <b>
                    =
                  </b>
                  <span>
                    (target speed − current speed)
                  </span>
                  <b>
                    /
                  </b>
                  <span>
                    time constant τ
                  </span>
                </div>
                <p>
                  The time constant, τ, is the time taken to complete about 63% of the total change after a step, provided the response is not still limited by slew rate. A large time constant means a slower response. In robotics, these effects create phase delay between a controller’s command and the robot’s actual motion, which can affect line following, trajectory tracking, stopping distance, and controller stability.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Do not confuse a slew-limited ramp with first-order lag. A ramp has an approximately bounded slope; a first-order response has a slope that starts larger and then reduces as it approaches the target. Use step size, sampling rate, rise and fall direction, and the 63% point to test which explanation is supported by the data.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Earlier exercises reduced each trial to one settled speed. This exercise must retain a time series. The supplied <code>update(...)</code> refreshes the encoders at a nominal 10 ms interval, while <code>publishTelemetry(...)</code> reports at a separate nominal 50 ms interval. Keep the step-generation logic independent from the reporting method so the same controller routine can later be transferred to the Digital Twin. The strength and visible form of any dynamic effect may vary between teams.
                </p>
              </div>
