          <article id="exercise-10" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">
              10
            </div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div>
                  <span class="concept-tag">
                    Constructive control · threshold compensation
                  </span>
                  <h2>
                    Mitigating the Deadband
                  </h2>
                </div>
                <span class="time-badge">
                  25–35 min
                </span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  You have now measured the command region in which a wheel does not move reliably. Use that evidence to design a small command transformation: a zero requested motion should remain zero, while a non-zero low-speed request should become a command that clears the measured threshold for the relevant wheel and direction.
                </p>
                <p>
                  This is a constructive exercise, not a replacement for the earlier measurements. Keep the original raw-command results. The purpose is to test whether a compensation based on those results produces more reliable low-speed motion without creating an unreasonably large jump in response.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Keep the correction as a small, clearly named calculation that produces a PWM before the existing <code>robot.setMotorPWM(...)</code> call. This separates a portable controller decision from whichever reporting method you use. The measured thresholds must come from your own earlier trials, and the improvement or side effects may vary between teams, wheels, and directions.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="exercise-grid">

                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Deadband mitigation guidance"><button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">Self-Directed Route</button><button class="context-tab" data-context="ai" role="tab" aria-selected="false">AI-Assisted Route</button></div>
                  <div class="context-panel active" data-panel="nonai">
<h3>
                    Investigation brief
                  </h3>
                  <ol>
                    <li><strong>Start from Exercise 3.</strong> Keep the raw deadband test unchanged. Create <code>testDeadbandCompensation(Robot_c &amp;robot, unsigned long now)</code> and a small helper calculation that maps a requested command to a compensated PWM.</li>
                    <li><strong>Select and implement the new test.</strong> Call only the compensation test from <code>update(...)</code>. Preserve an exact zero command, use your measured first-motion threshold for a non-zero request, and use separate values only where the left/right or forward/reverse evidence supports them.</li>
                    <li><strong>Run paired physical trials.</strong> Choose low-speed requests that were unreliable in the raw test. For each request, measure the encoder response without compensation and then with compensation, using identical timing and starting conditions.</li>
                    <li><strong>Calculate and plot.</strong> Record requested value, transformed PWM, count change, encoder speed, and whether sustained motion occurred. Plot raw and compensated response near zero and make the size of any discontinuity visible.</li>
                    <li><strong>Predict before testing.</strong> Predict a fresh low-speed result from the measured threshold, then test it. Check for unwanted motion at zero, a large jump immediately above zero, and sensitivity to threshold uncertainty.</li>
                    <li><strong>Check the outcome.</strong> Your code should keep measurement, compensation, and motor command roles visible; the robot may improve, overshoot, remain inconsistent, or expose a trade-off; and your evidence should compare raw and compensated results with uncertainty. The magnitude and appearance will vary between teams.</li>
                  </ol>
                  <strong>Pseudocode before C++</strong>
                  <pre><code>FUNCTION compensate(request, measured threshold):
    IF request is exactly zero: return zero
    OTHERWISE: map the request beyond the measured threshold

FOR each low-speed test request:
    measure the raw response
    measure the compensated response under matched conditions</code></pre>
                  <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="A zero preserving deadband compensation maps zero request to zero PWM, and a nonzero request beyond the measured threshold">
                    <span>compensated PWM</span><b>=</b><span>0 when request = 0; otherwise a signed value beyond the measured threshold</span>
                  </div>
                  <p><strong>Block-diagram placeholder:</strong> requested motion → zero-preserving compensation → <code>robot.setMotorPWM(...)</code> → encoder response.</p>
                  <p><a class="template-link placeholder-link" href="#" aria-disabled="true" onclick="return false;">Example Python plotting notebook — link to be added</a></p>
                  <strong>Expected outcome: a tested low-speed correction</strong>
                  <p>You should be able to show whether the correction makes low-speed motion more reliable and quantify the discontinuity or other trade-off it introduces.</p>
</div>
                  <div class="context-panel" data-panel="ai" hidden><strong>AI-assisted option</strong>
                  <p>Ask AI to help express your proposed piecewise mapping, but provide your own thresholds and require it to preserve an exact zero command. Check the resulting implementation against raw encoder data.</p>
<div class="prompt-snippet">“Help me express a zero-preserving deadband compensation rule from these measured thresholds. State every assumption and do not invent threshold values.”</div>
<span class="ai-reminder">Measurements select the thresholds; a fresh run tests the compensation.</span></div>
                </div>
                <div class="investigation exercise-evidence">
                  <div class="measure-row"><span><b>Use</b> measured first-motion thresholds</span><span><b>Predict</b> reliable low-speed motion</span><span><b>Compare</b> raw and compensated encoder results</span></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-11" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">
              11
            </div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div>
                  <span class="concept-tag">
                    Constructive control · matched wheel response
                  </span>
                  <h2>
                    Left/Right PWM Equalisation
                  </h2>
                </div>
                <span class="time-badge">
                  30–40 min
                </span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  Equal numerical PWM values need not produce equal encoder speeds. Use your measured response data to choose different left and right commands that aim for the same wheel rotation rate. This is a feed-forward correction: it uses a measured model before the motion takes place, rather than correcting an error during the run.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Use the response curves gathered with the same <code>Robot_c</code> interface to calculate a left and right PWM pair before sending one <code>robot.setMotorPWM(left_pwm, right_pwm)</code> call. Keep target-speed selection separate from data reporting so that this controller logic can be used with either the physical robot or the Digital Twin. The size and direction of any left/right mismatch may vary between teams.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="exercise-grid">

                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="PWM equalisation guidance"><button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">Self-Directed Route</button><button class="context-tab" data-context="ai" role="tab" aria-selected="false">AI-Assisted Route</button></div>
                  <div class="context-panel active" data-panel="nonai">
<h3>
                    Investigation brief
                  </h3>
                  <ol>
                    <li><strong>Start from the left and right response data.</strong> Keep those original tests and create <code>testWheelEqualisation(Robot_c &amp;robot, unsigned long now)</code>. Choose at least two target encoder speeds inside the reliable range shared by both wheels.</li>
                    <li><strong>Select and implement the new test.</strong> Call only this function from <code>update(...)</code>. Use each measured response curve, fit, or lookup table to select one PWM for the left wheel and one for the right. Retain the target, selected PWMs, and prediction.</li>
                    <li><strong>Run matched physical trials.</strong> At each target, first apply equal numerical PWM as a baseline, then apply the calculated unequal pair. Use identical settling and measurement intervals and calculate both encoder speeds.</li>
                    <li><strong>Calculate and plot.</strong> Report the left/right speed mismatch before and after equalisation, in counts per second and optionally as a percentage of target speed. Plot achieved left and right speeds against target.</li>
                    <li><strong>Validate the correction.</strong> Test a target not used to select the mapping. Decide whether one scale factor works across the range or whether the required correction changes with operating point or direction.</li>
                    <li><strong>Check the outcome.</strong> Your code should make the model-derived PWM pair visible; the robot may become better matched, remain different, or be over-corrected; and your evidence should compare baseline, prediction, achieved speeds, residual mismatch, and held-out performance. The magnitude and appearance will vary between teams.</li>
                  </ol>
                  <strong>Pseudocode before C++</strong>
                  <pre><code>FOR each target encoder speed:
    use the left response model to select left PWM
    use the right response model to select right PWM
    predict the remaining speed mismatch
    run the paired command and measure both wheel speeds
COMPARE against an equal-PWM baseline</code></pre>
                  <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Wheel speed mismatch equals left encoder speed minus right encoder speed">
                    <span>wheel-speed mismatch</span><b>=</b><span>left encoder speed − right encoder speed</span>
                  </div>
                  <p><strong>Block-diagram placeholder:</strong> common target speed → left inverse model and right inverse model → PWM pair → two encoder measurements → mismatch.</p>
                  <p><a class="template-link placeholder-link" href="#" aria-disabled="true" onclick="return false;">Example Python plotting notebook — link to be added</a></p>
                  <strong>Expected outcome: a validated feed-forward pairing</strong>
                  <p>You should finish with a transparent rule for choosing two commands that aim for one common speed, plus evidence showing where that rule succeeds and where it does not.</p>
</div>
                  <div class="context-panel" data-panel="ai" hidden><strong>AI-assisted option</strong>
                  <p>Ask AI to plot your two response curves or interpolate candidate commands. Independently check units, direction, and the data point held back for evaluation.</p>
<div class="prompt-snippet">“Given these labelled left and right encoder-speed measurements, estimate PWM values for a common target speed and identify one target to reserve for validation.”</div>
<span class="ai-reminder">The robot decides whether matched predicted speeds are achieved.</span></div>
                </div>
                <div class="investigation exercise-evidence">
                  <div class="measure-row"><span><b>Target</b> matched encoder speed</span><span><b>Choose</b> left/right PWM separately</span><span><b>Evaluate</b> residual speed mismatch</span></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-12" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">
              12
            </div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div>
                  <span class="concept-tag">
                    Constructive control · inverse response model
                  </span>
                  <h2>
                    Response linearisation
                  </h2>
                </div>
                <span class="time-badge">
                  35–45 min
                </span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  A linearised command interface aims to make equal changes in a requested speed produce approximately equal changes in measured encoder speed. Build an inverse response rule from your own command-versus-speed measurements: it may be a fitted curve, a piecewise rule, or a lookup table with interpolation. The rule should include the limits of the measured operating range rather than extrapolating without evidence.
                </p>
                <p>
                  This final exercise combines the same interface used throughout: a controller calculation selects PWM, <code>robot.setMotorPWM(...)</code> applies the request, and cached encoder readings test the physical result. Keep the inverse mapping simple enough to inspect and transfer to the Digital Twin. During Phase 1 you may report data in any clear form; during Phase 2, agree one telemetry record and unit convention with your team so physical and simulated runs can be compared consistently. The required mapping and remaining errors may vary between teams.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="exercise-grid">

                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Response linearisation guidance"><button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">Self-Directed Route</button><button class="context-tab" data-context="ai" role="tab" aria-selected="false">AI-Assisted Route</button></div>
                  <div class="context-panel active" data-panel="nonai">
<h3>
                    Investigation brief
                  </h3>
                  <ol>
                    <li><strong>Start from your measured response models.</strong> Keep the earlier tests and create <code>testResponseLinearisation(Robot_c &amp;robot, unsigned long now)</code>. Define the desired input in encoder counts per second and choose a fitted inverse, piecewise rule, or lookup table with interpolation.</li>
                    <li><strong>Select and implement the new test.</strong> Call only this function from <code>update(...)</code>. Put the desired-speed-to-PWM calculation in a clearly named helper, retain its measured valid range, and keep separate mappings only where wheel or direction evidence requires them.</li>
                    <li><strong>Run the physical trials.</strong> Choose evenly spaced desired speeds inside the measured range. For each target, calculate PWM, apply it through the established timed routine, and measure settled encoder speed. Reserve at least one speed or short sequence for evaluation.</li>
                    <li><strong>Calculate and plot.</strong> Plot desired speed against measured speed before and after linearisation. Calculate residual error and compare the slope and curvature of both plots. Show repeats or uncertainty rather than only a smooth fitted line.</li>
                    <li><strong>Validate and limit the model.</strong> Test the held-out target or sequence. State how the mapping behaves between measured points, at exact zero, near deadband and saturation, and outside the measured range; do not extrapolate silently.</li>
                    <li><strong>Check the outcome.</strong> Your code should expose a portable desired-speed interface and a bounded inverse mapping; the robot may become more linear while retaining scatter, delay, or limits; and your evidence should include the source model, before/after plot, residuals, held-out prediction, uncertainty, and validity range. The magnitude and appearance will vary between teams.</li>
                  </ol>
                  <strong>Pseudocode before C++</strong>
                  <pre><code>FUNCTION pwmForDesiredSpeed(desired speed):
    handle exact zero explicitly
    reject or clamp values outside the measured range
    invert the measured response using the chosen simple rule

FOR each desired speed:
    calculate PWM, run the settled-speed test, and store error
VALIDATE with the reserved target or sequence</code></pre>
                  <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Linearisation residual equals measured encoder speed minus desired encoder speed">
                    <span>linearisation residual</span><b>=</b><span>measured encoder speed − desired encoder speed</span>
                  </div>
                  <p><strong>Block-diagram placeholder:</strong> desired encoder speed → bounded inverse response model → PWM → robot or Digital Twin → measured encoder speed → residual.</p>
                  <p><a class="template-link placeholder-link" href="#" aria-disabled="true" onclick="return false;">Example Python plotting notebook — link to be added</a></p>
                  <strong>Expected outcome: a portable, evidence-bounded interface</strong>
                  <p>You should be able to request speed through one inspectable mapping, compare physical and simulated outcomes in common units, and state honestly where the mapping is unsupported.</p>
</div>
                  <div class="context-panel" data-panel="ai" hidden><strong>AI-assisted option</strong>
                  <p>Ask AI to compare a simple fit, piecewise rule, and lookup-table interpolation for your measurements. Require a plot of residuals and test the selected rule on held-back data.</p>
<div class="prompt-snippet">“Compare inverse-model options for these PWM and encoder-speed measurements. Do not extrapolate beyond the measured range, and show how to validate with held-back data.”</div>
<span class="ai-reminder">A smooth plot is not validation unless it predicts new measurements.</span></div>
                </div>
                <div class="investigation exercise-evidence">
                  <div class="measure-row"><span><b>Map</b> desired speed to PWM</span><span><b>Measure</b> realised encoder speed</span><span><b>Validate</b> linearity on held-back data</span></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
